description:
event.rs:824-828 — the filter deserializer for CalendarEvent/query:

  let filter: Option<CalendarEventFilterCondition> =
      match args.remove("filter").unwrap_or(Value::Null) {
          Value::Null => None,
          v => Some(serde_json::from_value(v).map_err(|_| JmapError::unsupported_filter())?),
      };

The .map_err(|_| ...) drops the serde_json::Error and replaces it with a generic 'unsupported_filter' code that carries no description.

Compare the sibling deserializers in the same handler — ids (event.rs:51-57), properties (event.rs:59-65), sort (event.rs:830-837) — they all preserve the description via JmapError::invalid_arguments(format!(...)):

  serde_json::from_value(v)
      .map_err(|_| JmapError::invalid_arguments("ids must be an Id array"))?

The inconsistency is real: a client sending a malformed filter gets 'unsupportedFilter' with no clue about which field was wrong, while a malformed sort or properties gets a useful 'must be an array' message. The simplest fix preserves both pieces of information:

  Some(serde_json::from_value(v).map_err(|e|
      JmapError::unsupported_filter_with_description(e.to_string())
  )?)

…or use invalid_arguments instead of unsupported_filter for non-FilterCondition shapes (a parsing error and a not-supported-clause are conceptually different — only a syntactically-valid FilterCondition with an unhandled property is 'unsupported').

The comment at event.rs:820-823 explains the dual-purpose of this branch ('any non-FilterCondition input falls through to unsupportedFilter — which simultaneously satisfies the §5.11 MUST be FilterCondition rule') but that justification still doesn't require dropping the serde error context.
